  <h2>Research</h2>

  <p>
    My research focuses on the intersection of hardware security and machine learning, specifically targeting the runtime detection of microarchitectural attacks like <strong>Spectre v1/v2</strong> and <strong>Prime+Probe</strong>. I aim to build energy-aware secure systems that detect threats at the hardware level without the massive overhead of traditional software mitigations.
  </p>

  <h3>Key Thrusts</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Microarchitectural Attack Detection</strong><br />
      Using Hardware Performance Counters (HPCs) to identify anomalous execution footprints caused by transient execution and cache side-channel attacks across different architectures (Intel, ARM, AMD).
    </li>

    <li>
      <strong>Advanced AI Models for Security</strong><br />
      Developing robust, lightweight ML pipelines for attack detection. A core part of my work involves using a <strong>Hybrid Source-Free Domain Adaptation (SHOT) based LSTM + XGBoost classification head</strong> to maintain high detection accuracy even in noisy, real-world environments.
    </li>

    <li>
      <strong>Open Data & Benchmarking</strong><br />
      Addressing the lack of standardized data in hardware security research. I compiled and open-sourced the <a href="https://ieee-dataport.org/documents/microarchitectural-attack-detection-dataset-spectre-v1-spectre-v2-and-benign-workloads" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Microarchitectural Attack Detection Dataset</a> on IEEE DataPort to help the community train and validate defenses against modern hardware exploits.
    </li>
  </ul>
</WorkLayout>
